er  people feel differently but when I'm in  language I start out with small snippets  four lines six lines 30 seconds and I  have to go to the one minute and then  the five minute and then the ten minute  and then the 20 minute and eventually to  a book if I'm always dealing with short  snippets I never really get going in the  language and and the first real book I  read in a language it's like climbing  Mount Everest it's a major achievement  and I think that we should encourage  people whether they're wanting to become  better at their own language or learning  another language to go to to spend more  time don't be distracted or you know  little messages coming in little Twitter  messages coming in sending little  messages so you write two or three  Twitter messages in Russian a day to  meet that's not as valuable as spending  an hour listening to very interesting  conversation in Russian so I still feel  Twitter as a distraction email or forums  but even there the you know yeah forums  are okay forums are okay and I must say  that I'm not normally inclined to write  in the language that I'm learning but  where we have our forums that link is  carrying on in Russian or whatever I'll  get on there that's motivates me to  write something and nowadays I can  because we have a spellchecker built  right in that really helps it on have to  worry about spelling mistakes and so  forth  so yeah writing I guess it's a good  thing but at least take a paragraph or  two and what I read I want to read the  longer when I get on you know if I read  a forum the one-liners in Roger I don't  even bother reading them I want to get a  meaty paragraph  and work my way through that so yeah  maybe I am saying that to our learning  styles so they're probably our learning  styles but I don't think the brain  learns fundamentally differently I think  they're people like different things  some people like poetry  some people like music some people learn  from you but I think if I liked learning  from music I probably could so all I'm  saying is I think the brain learns  essentially the same way but we have  different tastes and I think that the  where we are headed with with more  elearning or with more internet based  learning is allowing learners more  freedom to learn the way they like to  learn and so that it becomes less of an  issue for teachers to worry about it's  more a matter of making sure that there  is enough there for everybody's tastes  and enough support encouragement so that  what the learners go oh I take advantage  of this stuff so there you have it I  would have very much appreciate hearing  back thank you
 

